  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- EXERCISES -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercises xml:id="exer-descriptive-stats">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-mean-vs-median">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A conservation biologist measures the nesting success rate of 50
          Lesser Flamingo colonies across three lakes.
          Most colonies succeed at rates between 70–90%, but one isolated colony
          at Lake Bogoria succeeded at only 15% due to a drought.
          Which measure—mean or median—would better represent the typical
          nesting success?
          Explain your reasoning.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-cv-interpretation">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The mean bill depth of Lesser Flamingos is 16.4 mm with a standard
          deviation of 0.8 mm.
          For Greater Flamingos, the mean is 21.7 mm with a standard deviation
          of 1.2 mm.
          Which species shows greater relative variability in bill depth?
          Show your work.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-conditional-prop">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the lake cross-tabulation ( <xref ref="table-cross-tab-lakes"/>
          ):
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            What proportion of all 344 flamingos were captured at Lake
            Elementaita?
          </li>

          <li>
            Given that a flamingo was captured at Lake Elementaita, what is the
            probability it was a Greater Flamingo?
          </li>

          <li>Explain why your answers to (a) and (b) are different.</li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-missing-data-bias">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Seven flamingos have missing sex determinations in the dataset.
          Suppose all seven missing values are from birds over 3200 g (the
          largest birds captured).
          Would removing these seven rows introduce bias in the estimated mean
          body mass?
          Why or why not?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-histogram-interpretation">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Look at the body mass histogram ( <xref ref="fig-mass-histogram"/> ).
          Based on the visual, estimate:
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>How many distinct species does it suggest are present?</li>
          <li>Roughly where is the median body mass of the combined sample?</li>
          <li>
            Why would reporting a single mean body mass for all 336 birds be
            misleading?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>
